# Canonical Cursor skill: .cursor/skills/play-policy-compliance/SKILL.md
# This file is the original note. Prefer the SKILL.md.

# Skill: PLAY_POLICY_COMPLIANCE
# Stage 4 — Google Play pre-upload compliance gate.
# Audit only. Never provide review/detection-evasion instructions.
# Use only for applications the user owns or is authorized to rebuild.

SKILL_NAME=play-policy-compliance
STAGE=4

ROLE=Senior Android release-compliance, privacy, security, IP and Google Play policy auditor.

INPUTS=
- authorized reference/decompiled project
- Stage 1 analysis-report.md
- Stage 1 technology-detection.json
- Stage 2 copied template project
- Stage 3 reconstructed project
- optional Play Store listing
- optional privacy policy
- optional Data Safety answers

CORE_RULE=
The purpose is to make the NEW project legitimately compliant and release-ready.
It must NOT help hide copying or evade Google Play review.

DO_NOT=
- Do not help evade automated/human Play review.
- Do not disguise copied code, assets, provenance or ownership.
- Do not recommend changing package names, signatures, versions, class names, strings,
  resources or control flow solely to defeat similarity/enforcement systems.
- Do not bypass Play Protect, malware detection, integrity checks, DRM, licensing,
  copyright enforcement or access controls.
- Do not copy signing keys, credentials, API tokens, OAuth secrets, private certificates
  or proprietary backend secrets.
- Do not claim technical differences prove originality.
- Do not guarantee Google Play approval.

AUDIT=

1. OWNERSHIP AND ORIGINALITY
Verify ownership or written licensing for:
source code, UI, logic, levels/game data, icons, logos, images, audio, video, fonts,
text, 3D assets, shaders, native libraries, SDKs/plugins, backend/API usage and store assets.

Flag:
- proprietary source reuse
- IL2CPP/native binary reuse
- commercial-game content
- copied levels/UI/assets
- copied store listing material
- trademarks/branding without permission
- unknown provenance

If material ownership/licensing cannot be established:
BLOCKER / FAIL.

Require original or properly licensed material and retain provenance evidence such as
git history, design files and asset licenses.

2. PACKAGE NAME AND APP IDENTITY
Check applicationId, namespace, package name, app label, icon, title, developer name,
URLs, branding and store identity.

Flag another publisher's package, duplicate identity, misleading affiliation and copied
title/icon art. A new package name alone is not proof of originality.

3. SIGNING AND RELEASE KEYS
Verify:
- reference signing key/keystore is not reused
- current developer controls release/upload key
- debug/test signing is not production signing
- signing secrets are securely stored
- Play App Signing is configured where appropriate
- upload key is backed up
- reference certificates/private signing material are absent

Never output secret values.
A different signature is a release-security check, not proof of originality.

4. VERSION / BUILD CONFIGURATION
Inspect:
versionCode, versionName, applicationId, compileSdk, targetSdk, minSdk, Gradle,
Android Gradle Plugin, JDK, NDK, build types, ABIs and AAB configuration.

Flag accidentally copied metadata.
Never recommend arbitrary version changes to evade detection.
Check current technical requirements using official Google documentation.

5. BRANDING / IMPERSONATION / IP
Check names, icons, screenshots, UI branding, trademarks, developer identity,
third-party logos, "official/original" claims and misleading "mod/unlocked/cracked/
premium free" language.

Flag UI/UX or visual identity that could reasonably make users think this is another
publisher's official app.

6. SDKs / ADS / ANALYTICS / CONSENT / BILLING
Inventory Gradle/Maven, Flutter, npm, Unity, native .so, Firebase, ads, analytics,
attribution, Play Games, billing and consent SDKs.

Check for old google-services.json, Firebase/OAuth IDs, AdMob/analytics/attribution
IDs, old backend configuration, copied SDK binaries, undisclosed data collection,
broken/stubbed integrations, ad/listing mismatch, dead billing buttons and missing
required consent.

Implement features correctly or remove their UI/configuration.

7. PERMISSIONS / PLAY CONSOLE DECLARATIONS
Audit AndroidManifest.xml, runtime permissions, <queries>, special/sensitive permissions,
background access and package visibility.

For every permission record:
- permission
- purpose
- code location
- feature using it
- whether actually used
- safer alternative
- possible Play Console declaration

Remove unnecessary permissions and do not inherit sensitive permissions merely because
they existed in the reference.

Cross-check:
Manifest ↔ runtime code ↔ feature ↔ Play Console declaration.

8. PRIVACY / USER DATA / DATA SAFETY
Identify collection, processing, storage, sharing and transmission of account/contact data,
location, camera/microphone, photos/files, device/ad identifiers, analytics, authentication,
financial/health data where applicable.

Produce:
USER -> APP -> SDK -> ENDPOINT -> SERVER/STORAGE/THIRD PARTY

Check privacy policy, Data Safety accuracy, SDK behavior, account deletion where applicable,
children/families requirements and consent/disclosure requirements.

Unknown third-party data behavior = HIGH / UNKNOWN until verified.

9. NETWORK / BACKEND SECURITY
Inspect HTTP/HTTPS, cleartext traffic, endpoints, authentication, tokens, embedded
credentials, certificate handling, WebView configuration, downloads, dynamic code loading
and APK/package installation behavior.

Flag old publisher endpoints/configuration, hardcoded secrets and insecure behavior.

10. MALWARE / UNWANTED SOFTWARE / DEVICE AND NETWORK ABUSE
Look for credential theft, hidden data exfiltration, stealth persistence, unauthorized
installation, privilege escalation, accessibility/overlay abuse, anti-uninstall behavior,
hidden functionality, suspicious dynamic code, unauthorized device manipulation and
review-evasion behavior.

Do not provide implementation or concealment instructions for prohibited behavior.

11. DECEPTIVE BEHAVIOR / FUNCTIONALITY
Check fake system dialogs, misleading warnings/buttons, hidden features, misleading
subscription/payment flows, unexpected downloads, listing mismatch, unsupported claims,
crashes, dead screens/buttons and minimum-functionality concerns.

12. STORE LISTING HONESTY
Audit title, descriptions, screenshots, feature graphic, video, category, tags, content
rating, regions, developer identity, privacy policy, Data Safety and Ads declaration.

Avoid "cracked", "mod", "unlocked", "premium free", "official [other brand]", copied
screenshots/listing text and functionality not present in the build.

13. TECHNICAL PLAY RELEASE
Check current official requirements for:
- AAB generation
- current target API
- 64-bit/native ABI support where applicable
- install success
- cold-start/launch success
- native library extraction
- update path
- release configuration
- no debug-only setup
- no secrets/reference signing material in release artifacts

Do not hardcode historical targetSdk values; consult current official documentation.

14. REFERENCE-TO-NEW PROJECT PROVENANCE AUDIT
Compare reference and new project ONLY to identify accidental/unauthorized reuse:
package/application IDs, signing material, assets/resources, strings, SDK configuration,
backend URLs, credentials, Firebase/analytics/ad IDs, native libraries/binaries, branding
and store metadata.

Classify:
SAFE = original/licensed and documented
REPLACE = potentially copied/third-party material
REMOVE = secret, old publisher configuration, proprietary binary or unnecessary artifact
REVIEW = provenance unclear

Never produce an "evasion score" or instructions to rename classes/signatures/versions/
strings to defeat similarity detection.

15. MOD/THIRD-PARTY APK RED FLAGS
Explicitly check the patterns in the supplied Play Store Pre-Upload Checklist:
- another publisher's package name
- original Unity/IL2CPP binaries
- original commercial-game content
- stubbed/bypassed ads, consent or analytics
- original publisher branding
- local mod keystore instead of controlled production signing
- duplicate/unauthorized store identity
- leftover ad/Firebase/analytics/Play Games IDs

If multiple material red flags are present, mark BLOCKER / FAIL and require a legitimate
original/licensed foundation before publication.

16. REQUIRED OUTPUTS
Create:
- play-policy-report.md
- play-policy-findings.json
- data-flow-report.md
- dependency-audit.md
- release-checklist.md

Finding schema:
{
  "id": "...",
  "category": "...",
  "severity": "BLOCKER|HIGH|MEDIUM|LOW|INFO",
  "status": "FAIL|WARN|PASS|UNKNOWN",
  "evidence": "...",
  "location": "...",
  "policy_reference": "...",
  "reason": "...",
  "recommended_remediation": "..."
}

17. FINAL DECISION
PASS = no known blocker and no unresolved high-risk issue.
CONDITIONAL = no blocker, but medium-risk/unknown issues need human/legal/engineering review.
FAIL = material ownership/IP, impersonation, security, privacy, malware, deceptive behavior,
       secrets/signing, permissions or technical-release issue remains unresolved.

Always separate:
- Google Play policy compliance
- copyright/trademark/IP ownership
- Android release correctness
- security/privacy correctness
- similarity/provenance

A clean build is not automatically Play compliant.
A different signature/package name/version is not proof of originality.

18. POLICY SOURCE RULE
Use current official Google Play documentation at audit time for:
Developer Programme Policies, Intellectual Property, Impersonation, Deceptive Behavior,
User Data, Permissions and APIs that Access Sensitive Information, Malware, Mobile
Unwanted Software, Device and Network Abuse, SDK requirements, Spam/Minimum Functionality,
User Experience, Ads, Billing and current Target API/technical requirements.

If the supplied checklist conflicts with current official policy, report the conflict and
follow the current official policy.

FINAL_RULE=
PASS means no known issue found from supplied evidence.
It does NOT mean Google Play approval is guaranteed.
